---
/** Open distance scoring guide. Ported from react/pages/ScoringOpenDistance.tsx. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
---

<Base title="GlideComp - How Open Distance Scoring Works">
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground py-6 px-page-safe">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Open Distance Scoring Works
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Fly as far as you can — the simplest way to score a competition day
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li>
            <a href="#what-is-open-distance" class="hover:underline">
              What is Open Distance?
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#how-a-task-works" class="hover:underline">
              How a Task Works
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#how-distance-is-measured" class="hover:underline">
              How Your Distance is Measured
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#your-score" class="hover:underline">
              Your Score
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#glidecomp-notes" class="hover:underline">
              GlideComp Implementation Notes
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* What is Open Distance? */}
      <section id="what-is-open-distance" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What is Open Distance?</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Open distance is the simplest way to run a competition day. Everyone takes off from
            the same launch and flies as far as they can. There is no course and no goal. All that
            counts is how far you get from launch.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            This is the classic "free distance" or record-style format. Unlike
            <a href="/scoring/gap" class="underline hover:text-foreground">GAP scoring</a>, where
            pilots race a course of turnpoints to goal, open distance has no speed section, no
            turnpoints to tag, and no time, leading or arrival points. Your score is simply the
            distance you flew.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* How a Task Works */}
      <section id="how-a-task-works" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How a Task Works</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            An open-distance task has exactly one turnpoint: the <strong>Take-off</strong>, a
            cylinder around launch. Distance is measured from its edge. There is no start gate,
            no intermediate turnpoint and no goal.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            You launch, leave the take-off cylinder and fly. When you land, or the day ends, your
            flight is scored on how far you got from take-off.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* How Your Distance is Measured */}
      <section id="how-distance-is-measured" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How Your Distance is Measured</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Your scored distance is how far your <strong>furthest point</strong> is from the
            <strong>edge of the take-off cylinder</strong>, in a straight line:
          </p>
          <p class="bg-muted/50 rounded px-3 py-2 inline-block">
            Distance = distance from the take-off centre to your furthest point − cylinder radius
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">This means:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-muted-foreground space-y-2 ml-2">
            <li>
              <strong>The cylinder radius is not free distance.</strong> Distance starts at the
              cylinder's edge, not its centre. Where, or how often, you crossed that edge makes no
              difference.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Only getting further from launch counts.</strong> Circling in a thermal, or
              flying across the wind, adds nothing unless it takes you further away.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Your furthest point counts, not where you landed.</strong> If you reach a
              far point and then drift back towards launch before landing, you keep the far point.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Distances are measured on the WGS84 ellipsoid</strong>, the same geodesic
              maths GlideComp uses everywhere else, so they agree with the map.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A pilot who never leaves the take-off cylinder scores zero.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Your Score */}
      <section id="your-score" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Your Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Your score for the task is your distance in <strong>metres</strong>:
          </p>
          <p class="bg-muted/50 rounded px-3 py-2 inline-block">
            Score = metres of open distance flown
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Pilots are ranked by distance, furthest first. There is no 1000-point pool, no task
            validity, and no split into distance, time, leading and arrival; those all belong to
            GAP. The distance you flew <em>is</em> the score.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A penalty from the organiser is subtracted in metres. In a multi-day competition,
            your total is the sum of your task scores: the distance you flew across all the days.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* GlideComp Implementation Notes */}
      <section id="glidecomp-notes" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">GlideComp Implementation Notes</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Choose open distance in a competition's scoring settings, and every task in that
            competition is scored this way. Because the format has no course, each task must
            have exactly one turnpoint, the Take-off. GlideComp won't save a task with any other
            route while the competition is set to open distance.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Every scoring decision is explained and unit-tested, and the scoring engine is open
            source on
            <a
              href="https://github.com/pokle/glidecomp"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="underline hover:text-foreground"
            >GitHub</a>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Looking for the other format? See
            <a href="/scoring/gap" class="underline hover:text-foreground">how GAP scoring works</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
